rtical lines x=1,...,x=n-2. Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover columns n-1 and n. Column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. So we need to cover 3 points with 2 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so we can cover at most 2 points in column n-1 (one per line) and at most 2 points in column n (one per line). So we can cover all 3 points: assign one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1) maybe? That's a line through (n-1,1) and (n,1) horizontal, not sunny. So can't. So we need to assign each point to a separate line? Actually we have 2 sunny lines, we need to cover 3 points. So at least one line must cover two points. But any line covering two points in column n-1 and n must be either horizontal (if same y) or slope -1 (if sum constant). Let's check possibilities:\n\nPoints: (n-1,1),(n-1,2) in column n-1; (n,1) in column n.\n\nWe need to cover these three points with 2 sunny lines. Options:\n\n- One sunny line covers (n-1,1) and (n,1): slope = (1-1)/(n-(n-1)) = 0, not sunny. So can't.\n\n- One sunny line covers (n-1,2) and (n,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1, not sunny. So can't.\n\n- One sunny line covers (n-1,1) and (n-1,2): vertical, not sunny.\n\nThus any line covering two of these points is non-sunny. So we cannot cover all three points with only 2 sunny lines. So k=2 impossible. This matches the solution's argument.\n\nThus k=2 is indeed impossible.\n\nNow for k≥4: The solution claims that k≥4 is impossible. Let's test for k=4 for n=5 maybe. For n=5, we have 5 lines total, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line can be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. Let's see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines and 1 vertical line.\n\nColumns: 1 has 5 points, 2 has 4, 3 has 3, 4 has 2, 5 has 1. With 4 sunny lines, each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines. So column 1 (5 points) needs at least one non-sunny line covering at least one point. Since we have only one non-sunny line, it must be vertical line x=1 (o