ts in T_5. Points in T_5: (1,5) sum=6, (2,4) sum=6, (3,3) sum=6, (4,2) sum=6, (5,1) sum=6. So points on the line x + y = 6 are all points with sum=6. That's a line of slope -1 (parallel to x+y=0). That's not sunny. So we cannot use that. So we need sunny lines.\n\nLet's try slope -2: y = -2x + b. For x=1, y = b-2. For x=2, y = b-4. For x=3, y = b-6. For x=4, y = b-8. For x=5, y = b-10. We need y >= 1 and x+y <=6. So for x=1, y = b-2, sum = 1 + b - 2 = b - 1 <= 6 => b <= 7. Also y >= 1 => b >= 3. So b in [3,7]. For x=2, y = b-4, sum = 2 + b - 4 = b - 2 <= 6 => b <= 8. y >= 1 => b >= 5. So b in [5,8]. Intersection with previous: b in [5,7]. For x=3, y = b-6, sum = 3 + b - 6 = b - 3 <= 6 => b <= 9. y >= 1 => b >= 7. So b in [7,9]. Intersection with [5,7] => b = 7. So b=7 yields points (1,5), (2,3), (3,1). Check sums: (1,5) sum=6, (2,3) sum=5, (3,1) sum=4. All allowed. So line y = -2x + 7 contains three points in T_5: (1,5), (2,3), (3,1). Indeed that works. So slope -2 line can contain 3 points for k=5. So we have at least two lines: y = x (points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)) and y = -2x + 7 (points (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)). That's 6 points. T_5 has 15 points. We need 5 lines each covering 3 points, total 15 points. So we need 3 more lines each covering 3 points, disjoint from the previous ones. Are there other sunny lines that can contain 3 points? Possibly slope -1/2 lines? Let's test slope -1/2: y = -x/2 + b. For integer points, x must be even. So x = 2,4. For x=2, y = -1 + b => y = b-1. For x=4, y = -2 + b => y = b-2. For x=6, not allowed. So at most 2 points. So slope -1/2 cannot achieve 3 points. So maybe other slopes with |p+q|=2 also produce lines with 3 points. Let's examine p+q = 2 possibilities: p+q = 2 => p = 2 - q. Since q>0, p can be positive, zero, or negative. p cannot be 0 (sunny lines require p != 0). So q cannot be 2 (p=0). So q=1 => p=1 => slope = p/q = 1 => line y = x + c? Actually p x - q y = c => x - y = c => y = x - c. That's slope 1. Already considered. q=3 => 