nges by q + (2 - q) = 2, as expected. So the sum increments by 2 each step. So the points in T_k have sum between 2 and k+1 inclusive. So the number of points is at most floor((k+1 - 2)/2) + 1 = floor((k-1)/2) + 1 = floor((k+1)/2). So the bound is reachable if we can find a line where the sum values are exactly the allowed sums: start at some sum s0 >= 2, then s0 + 2t <= k+1. So we need t from 0 to floor((k+1 - s0)/2). So number of points = floor((k+1 - s0)/2) + 1. To achieve the maximum floor((k+1)/2), we need s0 = 2 (the smallest possible sum). So we need a line that contains a point with sum = 2 (i.e., (1,1)). Then the line will contain points with sums 2,4,6,... up to <= k+1. For odd k, k+1 is even, so the last sum is k+1 (which is even). So the number of points is (k+1)/2. So we need a line that passes through (1,1) and has p+q = 2. Let's find p,q such that line passes through (1,1). The line equation p x - q y = c. For (1,1), c = p - q. So the line is p x - q y = p - q. This can be rearranged: p (x - 1) = q (y - 1). So (x - 1) / q = (y - 1) / p. So the line passes through (1,1) and has direction vector (q,p). So the line is parametric: (x,y) = (1 + q t, 1 + p t). So indeed the line passes through (1,1). So any p,q with p+q = 2 yields a line through (1,1). So we can choose any q != 2, p = 2 - q. For q=1, p=1 => line y = x (slope 1). For q=3, p=-1 => line direction (3,-1). That line passes through (1,1) and then points (4,0) (but y=0 not allowed), (7,-1) etc. So only (1,1) is in T_k? Actually (1,1) sum=2, next point sum=4: (1+3,1-1) = (4,0) sum=4 but y=0 not allowed (positive integer). So that line does not contain any other points in T_k. So it only contains (1,1). So it does not achieve the bound. So the bound is not automatically achieved for all p,q with p+q=2; we need the line to stay within positive integer region. So we need q and p such that the line stays within the region x>=1, y>=1 for the required number of steps. For q=1, p=1, the line (1+ t, 1+ t) 