eed to choose a set V of columns (size n-3) for which we place vertical lines. The remaining columns (size 3) will be covered by the two sunny lines. Let's denote the set of columns without vertical lines as C, with |C| = 3. In each column a in C, we need to cover the points (a,b) for b = 2,...,n+1-a using the two sunny lines. Since each sunny line can intersect column a at at most one point, across two sunny lines we can cover at most 2 points in column a. So we need that the number of points left in column a (i.e., n-a) is at most 2. So we need n-a <= 2 => a >= n-2. So the columns without vertical lines must be among the last three columns: a = n-2, n-1, n. But column n is already fully covered by horizontal line, so we don't need to consider it. So we need to choose columns a = n-2 and n-1 as the two columns without vertical lines (maybe also column n-2, n-1, and maybe some other column with n-a <= 2). Let's check column n-2: n - (n-2) = 2 points left (b = 2,...,n+1-(n-2) = 3?). Actually column n-2 originally has points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). After horizontal line y=1, we have (n-2,2),(n-2,3) left: 2 points. Column n-1 originally has points (n-1,1),(n-1,2). After horizontal line, we have (n-1,2) left: 1 point. Column n originally has only (n,1) which is covered. So we have columns n-2 and n-1 left without vertical lines. That's 2 columns, not 3. But we need exactly n-3 vertical lines, leaving 3 columns without vertical lines (since total columns = n). However, we can also leave column n (which is already covered by horizontal line) without a vertical line, but that's okay because it's already covered. So we can have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 vertical lines). Then columns n-2, n-1, n have no vertical lines. Column n is covered by horizontal line. Columns n-2 and n-1 need to be covered by the two sunny lines. Let's see if we can cover the remaining points in those columns with two sunny lines.\n\nColumn n-2: points left after horizontal lin