1 covers (n-3,1). Diagonal x+y=n covers (n-3,3) (since n-3 + 3 = n). So (n-3,2) remains uncovered.\n\n- Column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2). Horizontal covers (n-2,1). Diagonal covers (n-2,2) (since n-2 + 2 = n). So column n-2 fully covered.\n\n- Column n-1: (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Horizontal covers (n-1,1). Diagonal covers (n-1,1)? Actually diagonal x+y=n gives (n-1,1) (since n-1+1=n). So (n-1,2) remains uncovered.\n\n- Column n: (n,1). Horizontal covers (n,1). So column n fully covered.\n\nThus uncovered points: (n-3,2) and (n-1,2). That's 2 points in columns n-3 and n-1. We have 2 sunny lines left, so we can assign one sunny line to each point. That's fine. So we have a construction for any n >= 4? Let's check n=4: n-4 = 0 vertical lines, horizontal y=1, diagonal x+y=4 (since n=4). Then uncovered points: (n-3,2) = (1,2) and (n-1,2) = (3,2). So uncovered points are (1,2) and (3,2). Use two sunny lines: one through (1,2) (any slope not 0, infinite, -1), e.g., line through (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1, sunny. Another through (3,2) and (4,3) slope 1, sunny. So we have total lines: horizontal y=1, diagonal x+y=4, sunny line L1, sunny line L2. That's 4 lines, exactly k=2 sunny lines. Does this cover all points for n=4? Let's list points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). Horizontal y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Diagonal x+y=4 covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). Actually (3,1) already covered. So diagonal covers (1,3),(2,2). Sunny line L1: through (1,2) and (2,3) covers (1,2),(2,3). Sunny line L2: through (3,2) and (4,3) covers (3,2) and (4,3) but (4,3) is not in region (4+3=7 >5). So L2 only covers (3,2). So covered points: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1) from horizontal; (1,3),(2,2) from diagonal; (1,2),(2,3) from L1; (3,2) from L2. That's (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). Missing point: (1,4). Indeed (1,4) is not covered. Did we cover (1,4)? Horizontal y=1 doesn't. Diagonal x+y=4 doesn't (1+4=5). L1 passes through (1,2) and (2,3), not (1,4). L2 passes